    <section class="cooper-feature">
      <div class="cooper-copy">
        <p class="eyebrow">A LITERARY DOORWAY INTO THE PAST</p>
        <h2>From Fenimore Cooper to the history behind the stories</h2>
        <p>For generations of readers, James Fenimore Cooper's <em>The Last of the Mohicans</em> opened a window onto the forests and frontier conflicts of eighteenth-century North America. Chingachgook and Uncas became unforgettable literary figures. We can keep the sense of adventure that made these stories memorable while learning to distinguish fiction from the lives and histories of real Native nations.</p>
        <p class="ru">Для многих поколений читателей романы Фенимора Купера стали первым знакомством с миром коренных народов Северной Америки. Чингачгук и Ункас — яркие литературные герои, но роман не является документальным описанием жизни всех индейских народов. Мы сохраним любовь к приключениям и одновременно откроем подлинную историю.</p>
        <div class="cooper-links"><a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/files/940/940-h/940-h.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read the novel · Project Gutenberg ↗</a><a href="https://www.neh.gov/humanities/2007/septemberoctober/feature/wyeth%E2%80%99s-noble-savage" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">How art shaped the image of Uncas · NEH ↗</a><a href="https://twain.lib.virginia.edu/projects/rissetto/last.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">A literary analysis · University of Virginia ↗</a></div>
      </div>
      <div class="cooper-note"><span>READ WITH TWO LENSES</span><strong>The story</strong><p>Adventure, friendship, courage, loss, and the imagination of a nineteenth-century novelist.</p><strong>The history</strong><p>Specific Native nations, their own voices, documented events, and histories that continue today.</p></div>
    </section>


    <section class="osceola-feature">
      <div class="osceola-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">ANOTHER CLASSIC OF FRONTIER LITERATURE</p>
        <h2>Osceola — the Seminole story</h2>
        <p>Mayne Reid's adventure novel <em>Osceola the Seminole; or, The Red Fawn of the Flower Land</em> was first published in 1859. It brought the Seminole struggle in Florida into the world of popular adventure fiction. The complete text is available through Project Gutenberg.</p>
        <p class="ru">Роман Майн Рида «Оцеола, вождь семинолов» (английское название — <em>Osceola the Seminole; or, The Red Fawn of the Flower Land</em>) впервые вышел в 1859 году. Он переносит читателя во Флориду и в эпоху борьбы семинолов за родную землю.</p>
        <a class="osceola-book-link" href="https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/35620" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read Mayne Reid's novel · Project Gutenberg ↗</a>
      </div>
      <div class="osceola-history">
        <span>THE HISTORICAL PERSON</span>
        <h3>Osceola (Asi-yaholo)</h3>
        <p>Osceola was an influential Seminole war leader during the Second Seminole War (1835–1842). He was captured by U.S. forces in 1837 while meeting under a flag of truce and died in captivity at Fort Moultrie on January 30, 1838.</p>
        <p class="osceola-correction">A useful historical correction: although often called “Chief Osceola” in popular culture, he was not a Seminole chief. He was an important warrior and resistance leader. The Seminole struggle continued after his death, under many other leaders.</p>
        <a href="https://www.nps.gov/people/osceola.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">National Park Service: Osceola ↗</a>
        <a href="https://www.fsu.edu/seminole-tribe/history" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Seminole history · Florida State University ↗</a>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="london-feature">
      <div class="london-intro">
        <p class="eyebrow">THE FAR NORTH IN AMERICAN LITERATURE</p>
        <h2>Jack London — stories from the North</h2>
        <p>In <em>Children of the Frost</em> (1902), Jack London turned to Indigenous life in the northern regions of North America. The collection brings together ten stories, including <em>The Law of Life</em>, <em>Keesh, the Son of Keesh</em>, and <em>The League of the Old Men</em>.</p>
        <p class="ru">В сборнике «Дети мороза» (1902) Джек Лондон обращается к жизни коренных народов Севера. Среди десяти рассказов — «Закон жизни», «Киш, сын Киша» и «Лига стариков». Это уже другой мир: северные леса, Юкон, суровый климат и столкновение традиционного уклада с быстро меняющейся эпохой.</p>
        <a class="london-book-link" href="https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/10736" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Read <em>Children of the Frost</em> · Project Gutenberg ↗</a>
      </div>
      <div class="london-stories">
        <article><span>01 · THE LAW OF LIFE</span><h3>An old man's final winter</h3><p>An elderly man, Koskoosh, is left behind as his community moves on. The story explores age, survival, memory, and the harshness of life in the Arctic.</p></article>
        <article><span>02 · THE LEAGUE OF THE OLD MEN</span><h3>A voice from the Whitefish people</h3><p>Imber, an old man from the Whitefish River, explains the violence between his people and incoming settlers. The story raises questions about conquest, law, justice, and whose history gets heard.</p></article>
        <article><span>03 · KEESH, THE SON OF KEESH</span><h3>Ingenuity and leadership</h3><p>A young boy earns respect through intelligence and practical skill. It offers a different kind of northern adventure, centred on resourcefulness rather than brute strength.</p></article>
      </div>
      <p class="london-note">Read with historical care: these are works of fiction written in the early twentieth century, not documentary accounts or substitutes for the voices of the specific Indigenous nations whose homelands are represented.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="native-section">
      <p class="eyebrow">WORDS AND IDENTITY</p>
      <h2>Who are Indigenous peoples?</h2>
      <p>In the United States, readers may encounter the terms <em>Native American</em>, <em>American Indian</em>, <em>Alaska Native</em>, and <em>Indigenous</em>. Preferences differ among individuals and nations. When possible, use the name a person or community uses for itself — for example, Diné, Anishinaabe, Haudenosaunee, or Cherokee — rather than assuming that one broad label fits everyone.</p>
      <p>Many communities also use the word <em>Nation</em> because it recognizes their distinct political, cultural, and historical identity. A tribal nation is not simply a cultural club or an ethnic label: federally recognized tribes have a government-to-government relationship with the United States and exercise forms of self-government.</p>
      <p class="ru">В США используются названия Native American, American Indian, Alaska Native и Indigenous, однако предпочтения различаются. Если возможно, лучше называть конкретный народ его собственным именем. Многие сообщества используют слово Nation, подчёркивая свою историческую и политическую самостоятельность.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="native-section">
      <p class="eyebrow">BEFORE THE UNITED STATES</p>
      <h2>North America before colonization</h2>
      <p>Indigenous societies were not isolated or technologically static. Across the continent, people built large settlements and cities, cultivated crops, managed forests and waterways, developed sophisticated fishing and hunting systems, made ceramics and textiles, and maintained long-distance trade routes. Communities adapted their homes, food systems, and technologies to very different environments — from Arctic coasts and forests to deserts, river valleys, plains, and the Pacific Northwest.</p>
      <div class="fact-grid">
        <article><h3>Agriculture & engineering</h3><p>Maize, beans, and squash supported many farming communities. In some regions, people built irrigation systems, raised fields, terraces, and extensive earthworks.</p></article>
        <article><h3>Cities & networks</h3><p>Places such as Cahokia were major population and ceremonial centres, connected to wider networks of exchange and influence.</p></article>
        <article><h3>Trade & knowledge</h3><p>Materials, food, tools, designs, and knowledge travelled over long distances through networks maintained by Indigenous communities.</p></article>
        <article><h3>Many environments</h3><p>There was no single way of life: coastal fishing villages, agricultural towns, mobile hunting communities, and desert settlements all had distinct histories.</p></article>
      </div>
      <p class="ru">Коренные общества занимались земледелием, инженерным строительством, торговлей, рыболовством и ремёслами. Существовали крупные города и развитые сети обмена. Образ жизни зависел от местной природы и не был одинаковым на всём континенте.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="native-section">
      <p class="eyebrow">A CONTINENT OF HOMELANDS</p>
      <h2>Different regions, different histories</h2>
      <p>Broad cultural regions can help us begin to understand the continent, but they are not rigid borders and should never replace the names and histories of individual nations.</p>
      <div class="region-grid">
        <article><h3>Northeast & Great Lakes</h3><p>Homelands of many Algonquian- and Iroquoian-speaking peoples, including nations of the Haudenosaunee Confederacy and many Anishinaabe communities.</p></article>
        <article><h3>Southeast</h3><p>Homelands of nations including the Cherokee, Muscogee (Creek), Choctaw, Chickasaw, and Seminole, with varied towns, farming systems, diplomacy, and trade.</p></article>
        <article><h3>Great Plains</h3><p>Many nations developed distinct lifeways shaped by grasslands, river systems, trade, and — in later centuries — the horse. Plains regalia is culturally specific, not universal Native clothing.</p></article>
        <article><h3>Southwest</h3><p>Diné (Navajo), Hopi, Pueblo communities, Apache nations, and others have deep histories in a region shaped by farming, architecture, weaving, trade, and diverse languages.</p></article>
        <article><h3>California & Pacific Coast</h3><p>Hundreds of distinct communities developed knowledge systems and lifeways connected to coastal, river, forest, and inland environments.</p></article>
        <article><h3>Northwest Coast, Alaska & Subarctic</h3><p>Coastal and northern peoples developed cultures shaped by salmon, marine resources, forests, ice, and extensive knowledge of local environments.</p></article>
      </div>
      <p class="ru">Региональные карты помогают увидеть разнообразие, но не должны подменять историю конкретных народов. Границы культурных областей условны и менялись со временем.</p>
      <div class="language-map-feature">
        <div class="language-map-heading">
